{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>A small city.</b> The early commentators said that this is by way of parable: the small city is the body of man, and the few people in it are the natural powers, servants of the soul, and the great king is the evil inclination, and the poor man is the intellect. But the correct view is that it is not by way of parable, but as its literal sense, and its meaning concerns the wise man who has no bread — he himself, how poor he is, despite knowing — even though his wisdom does not benefit him in this world in the majority of cases. There are also times when his wisdom benefits more than kingship. He said a small city, corresponding to and a great king surrounded it with a great army, and few people in it, and it is in a low place, so that one could build against it siege-works and ramparts higher than it, and there is no doubt it will be captured, with none to deliver." "<b>And the word מצודים.</b> Like למצד דוד מצודת ציון (2 Samuel 5:7). The mem is a root letter."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The phrase והוא בעצמו מה לעני יודע is compressed and textually difficult; rendered hyper-literally without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:44.967273980Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}